<BaseLayout
  title="Mobility Resilience — Radley Ciego"
  description="Causal inference framework estimating extreme weather impacts on NYC rideshare demand with equity stratification."
>
  <article>
    <div class="page-header">
      <h1>Mobility Resilience Under Extreme Weather</h1>
      <div class="page-header-subtitle">
        <a href="https://github.com/radleyciego/mobility-resilience-weather">GitHub &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Research Question</h2>
    <p>
      How does extreme precipitation affect rideshare demand across New York City neighborhoods,
      and do lower-income communities experience differential recovery trajectories?
    </p>

    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <p>
      A Difference-in-Differences causal framework treating extreme precipitation events as a natural
      experiment. The model estimates treatment effects using zone and time fixed effects, comparing
      trip volumes on weather-shock days against otherwise similar non-shock days within each taxi zone.
    </p>

    <h2>Data Pipeline</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>100M+ NYC TLC HVFHV trip records (Parquet, Polars) across 262 NYC taxi zones</li>
      <li>NOAA hourly precipitation observations merged at the zone-event level</li>
      <li>Automated pipeline: raw loading &rarr; datetime normalization &rarr; weather merge &rarr; feature engineering</li>
      <li>Calendar features (hour, day-of-week, month) and lagged demand (1-7 day lags) as controls</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Key Results</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Causal effect:</strong> Statistically significant +97 trip/zone/day demand increase
        on extreme precipitation days (p &lt; 0.0001)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Demand forecasting:</strong> XGBoost/LightGBM models achieved R&sup2; = 0.96 with
        time-series cross-validation
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Top predictors:</strong> Lagged demand (1-day and 7-day) and precipitation intensity
        identified via SHAP feature importance
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Equity analysis:</strong> Stratified by neighborhood income quintile with
        Mann-Whitney U testing for differential weather shock recovery
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tech Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Python, Polars, Pandas, GeoPandas, NumPy, SciPy, Statsmodels, Scikit-learn, XGBoost, LightGBM,
      SHAP, Matplotlib, NOAA API, NYC TLC data. Build tooling via uv + pyproject.toml.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
